Read §§1–2. **The proposed costs are defensible with extraction qualifications, but the literature gives substantially stronger special-family bounds than the draft uses.** Below, KNOWN means the cited statement was located; UNSURE identifies an unverified claim or qualification; NOT FOUND is a search result, not a novelty claim.

**1. Harman–Lewis narrow sectors — KNOWN.**

[Harman–Lewis, Theorem 2, p.119](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/4541183C02D39FD651979931A1B3B302/S0025579300014388a.pdf/gaussian-primes-in-narrow-sectors.pdf) states that, for sufficiently large \(X\), uniformly for
\[
0\le\alpha\le\pi/2,\qquad X^{-0.381}\le\Delta\le\pi/2,
\]
the number of Gaussian primes satisfying
\[
\alpha\le\arg\pi<\alpha+\Delta,\qquad |\pi|^2\le X
\]
exceeds \(c\Delta X/\log X\), with absolute \(c>0\).

This is a lower bound of the expected order, **not an asymptotic formula**. The exponent refers to **norm**:
\[
\Delta=X^{-0.381}=|z|^{-0.762}.
\]
The displayed theorem says “norm \(\le X\)”; the proof actually sieves the dyadic region \(X/2<|\pi|^2<X\) (§2, p.121, continuing p.123), so the stronger localization is available.

The axis sector gives \(p=a^2+b^2\), \(b\le p^{0.119}\). Your costs \(p^{0.3095+o(1)}\), hence balanced \(N^{0.15475+o(1)}\), are correct.

**NOT FOUND:** a 2015–2026 improvement of \(0.381\) for **every sector position**. Almost-all-sector results do not establish a guarantee at a prescribed angle.

**2. Discs, squares and polar boxes — KNOWN, with a newer UNSURE record claim.**

[Harman–Kumchev–Lewis, Theorem 1 and its Corollary](https://tigerweb.towson.edu/akumchev/a13.pdf) prove, for **every** \(z_0\in\mathbb C\),
\[
|\pi-z_0|\ll |z_0|^{0.53}+1.
\]
Thus, at norm \(X\), the disc—or a containing square—has area \(O(X^{0.53})\). A public center \(2^k(1+i)\) works. The radius includes an implied constant; a literal unit-constant bound follows with exponent \(0.53+\varepsilon\).

Their **Theorem 2** gives a correct-order lower bound \(\gg\phi y/\log X\) in any ideal class, uniformly in angular position, for
\[
X-y\le\mathrm N\mathfrak p<X,\quad
y\ge X^{0.765},\quad \phi\ge X^{-0.235}.
\]
Here \(\phi\) is the argument of their unit-invariant Hecke character; this changes Gaussian angular widths only by a constant. Minimum area is \(X^{0.53}\). Their introduction records Coleman’s earlier exponents \(7/12+\varepsilon\) and \(11/20\).

**KNOWN attribution; NOT FOUND exact thesis theorem number:** Lewis improved \(0.53\) to \(0.528\), according to [Järviniemi–Teräväinen, §1.1](https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/37565). I did not retrieve the thesis itself.

**UNSURE—stronger preprint:** [Runbo Li, arXiv:2308.04458v8, Theorem 8](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2308.04458v8), states the every-center exponent **\(0.5248\)**. I verified the statement, not its numerical sieve proof or refereed publication status.

These give balanced factoring exponents \(0.1325\), \(0.132\), and provisionally \(0.1312\), respectively.

For more flexible polar boxes, [Stucky, Theorem 1.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2008.11325), proves an asymptotic when
\[
y=X^\alpha,\quad \alpha>7/10,\quad \Delta y\ge X^{7/10+\varepsilon}.
\]
This does not improve Harman–Lewis’s full-annulus angular exponent.

**3. Prime-producing thin families — KNOWN.**

[Maynard, Theorems 1.1–1.2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1507.05080), has exactly:

- **Theorem 1.1:** arbitrary monic irreducible degree-\(n\) defining polynomial, \(n\ge4k\): asymptotic prime count in \([1,T]^{n-k}\).
- **Theorem 1.2:** \(K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt[n]{\vartheta})\), with \(X^n-\vartheta\) irreducible, \(n\ge22k/7\): correct-order lower bound. This is **not restricted to \(\vartheta=2\)**.

Write \(V=p^{v+o(1)}\); the proposed search costs \(p^{v/2+o(1)}\):

